# Each phrase promises a change for a later version, which the docs
# cannot keep current. The issue tracker holds the plan, and the docs
# describe what ships.
#
# Three phrases keep a runtime sense behind a guard. `in the future`
# skips a form of `be` or a time noun before it (`the timestamp must
# be in the future`, `a cutoff in the future`). The filter narrows the
# finding to the phrase itself, and a glued comma has its own branch
# so the sentence opener (`In the future, ...`) still counts once.
# `is planned` skips an agent (`the query is planned by the
# optimizer`), with a clause-end twin because a negated word after a
# space needs a word to consume. `to be implemented` needs `yet`,
# `still`, or `remains` before it, because the bare infinitive names a
# capability (`allows a filesystem to be implemented in user space`).
expr when [be, is, are, was, were, time, date, point, timestamp, deadline, cutoff, moment, instant, far, further]
expr main [(going forward), <(!@when in the future!,), (in the future)>, (in the future,), (in a future release), (in a future version), (in an upcoming release), (in upcoming releases), (in future releases), (in future versions), (will be added), (will be supported), (will be removed), (will be deprecated), ([is, are] planned !by), ([is, are] planned!( )), (on the roadmap), (coming soon), (not yet implemented), (not yet supported), ([yet, still, remains, remain] to be implemented), (will soon), (soon be)]
let message "Roadmap promise. Describe the system as it ships, and keep plans in the issue tracker."
let description "Flags phrases that promise a change for a later version."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "The parser supports tables."
allows "The timestamp must be in the future."
allows "A since cutoff in the future excludes the row."
allows "The query is planned by the optimizer."
allows "The library allows a filesystem to be implemented in user space."
